{
    "title": "Ensuring a National Policy Framework for Artificial Intelligence",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 16, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-16/pdf/2025-23092.pdf",
    "summary": "The President has signed an executive order to push a single, nationwide approach to artificial intelligence and limit state rules that the administration says slow innovation. It lays out legal, funding, and regulatory steps to favor one national standard over a patchwork of different state policies.\n\nWhat\u2019s in it: A new Justice Department task force will challenge state AI laws seen as burdensome or conflicting with federal policy. The Commerce Department will review state AI laws within 90 days and flag those that, in its view, force AI systems to change truthful outputs or require disclosures that could conflict with free speech. Some federal broadband and other discretionary funds could be conditioned on states not enforcing such laws. The federal communications regulator is asked to consider a national reporting and disclosure rule for AI that would override state requirements. The consumer protection agency will issue guidance on when state rules that mandate deceptive or altered AI outputs would be barred under federal law. The White House will also propose legislation to establish a uniform federal AI framework, while not overriding state laws on child safety, state use and buying of AI, and most rules about data centers and computing infrastructure.\n\nWho could benefit: AI companies, including startups, may face fewer conflicting requirements and lower compliance costs if one nationwide rulebook replaces many state-level ones. Investors and firms building AI-enabled services could see clearer paths to scale across the country. Supporters also point to potential gains for network buildouts and AI applications that rely on high-speed connectivity. People who favor faster AI adoption may welcome a simpler regulatory environment.\n\nWho might be negatively affected: States that want stronger rules on fairness, transparency, or accountability could see their authority limited and may risk losing some federal funds if they keep laws the administration deems onerous. Civil rights, consumer, and privacy advocates might worry that curbing state protections could weaken safeguards against discrimination, deceptive outputs, and misuse. Researchers, journalists, and watchdogs who rely on disclosure rules could also be affected. The approach is likely to trigger court fights, creating near-term uncertainty for governments and businesses.\n\nWhat to watch: Rapid agency actions over the next 30 to 90 days, potential lawsuits between the federal government and states, and the administration\u2019s legislative proposal for a national AI framework. The central debate will be how to balance speed and consistency for innovation with meaningful protections for people and communities.",
